%T A398280 25,26,27,29,39,30,31,32,33,34,35,36,37,38,48,40,41,42,43,44,45,46,47,
%U A398280 49,59,50,51,52,53,54,55,56,57,58,68,60,61,62,63,64,65,66
%N A398280 Lexicographically earliest sequence of distinct nonnegative integers such that the absolute difference of two consecutive terms can be computed with carries.
%C A398280 This sequence is a permutation of the nonnegative integers with inverse A398281:
%C A398280 - the sequence is not empty,
%C A398280 - the sequence can always be extended with a value of the form 10^k-1 strictly greater than the largest value so far,
%C A398280 - the sequence is infinite and unbounded,
%C A398280 - for any k >= 0, the first value >= 10^k necessarily appears beyond the value 10^k-1,
%C A398280 - all values of the form 10^k-1 appear,
%C A398280 - 10^k-1 is necessarily followed by the least value < 10^k-1 not yet in the sequence if any,
%C A398280 - so every integer will eventually appear.
%C A398280 This sequence first differs from A118763 for n = 101: a(101) = 100 while A118763(101) = 108.
%H A398280 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A398280/b398280.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..10000</a>
%H A398280 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A398280/a398280.gp.txt">PARI program</a>
%H A398280 <a href="/index/Per#IntegerPermutation">Index entries for sequences that are permutations of the natural numbers</a>
